\date{2025-11-13T17:54:23Z}
\title{Capabilities #{\to} devices oracle}

\import{swarm-macros}

\p{Of course we can invert the [\caps function](001F) to yield a function #{\caps^{-1} : \capset \to \powerset{\entset}} which maps a [capability](001D) to the set of [entities](001C) which [provide](001E) it.  More interesting, however, is that we also assume the existence of a \em{provisioning oracle} ##{\devs : \powerset{\capset} \to \powerset{\entset}} which calculates a set of devices which together provide (at least) the given set of capabilities.  That is, it must be the case that for any capability set #{\Delta}, ##{\caps(\devs(\Delta)) \supseteq \Delta.} Simply defining ##{\devs(\Delta) = \bigcup_{c \in \Delta} \caps^{-1}(c)} would fit the bill, but would be unsatisfying, since its output would typically contain many redundant devices.  Ideally we want some sort of "minimal" device set for a given set of capabilities.  Solving this exactly is [NP-hard](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Set_cover_problem), but there are various [heuristics](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/blob/c6f861d694cc528c6fa2639c7930d6367c4251f0/src/swarm-util/Swarm/Util.hs#L650-L704) that can give approximate solutions.  The precise algorithm used by the provisioning oracle does not matter; we simply assume that it exists and has the required property.}